Implemented Vercel Web Analytics for Next.js App Router project

## What was implemented:

### 1. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/analytics` version 2.0.1 using npm
- Package added to dependencies in package.json

### 2. Analytics Component Integration
- Added Analytics import in app/layout.tsx: `import { Analytics } from "@vercel/analytics/next"`
- Placed `<Analytics />` component within the body element of the root layout
- Followed official Vercel documentation for Next.js App Router implementation

### 3. Files Modified:
- `frontend/app/layout.tsx` - Added Analytics component import and placement
- `frontend/package.json` - Added @vercel/analytics dependency
- `frontend/package-lock.json` - Updated with new dependency tree

## Implementation Details:

According to the latest Vercel Web Analytics documentation (fetched from https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart):
- Used the Next.js App Router specific import: `@vercel/analytics/next`
- Placed the Analytics component in the root layout file as recommended
- The component is placed at the end of the body element to track page views

## Build & Testing:
- ✅ Build completed successfully with `npm run build`
- ✅ TypeScript compilation passed
- ✅ No linting errors introduced in modified files
- ✅ Lock files updated properly

## Next Steps:
To enable analytics tracking:
1. Deploy the application to Vercel
2. Enable Web Analytics in the Vercel dashboard (Project Settings > Analytics)
3. Verify tracking by checking the browser Network tab for `/_vercel/insights/*` requests after deployment

The implementation follows best practices and maintains the existing code structure.
